SM(607,{"n":"Revelation Introduction","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Revelation Introduction.mp3","d":2778.4,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.64,"The Revelation of St."],[22.0,"John. A completely non-controversial book that I'm sure we will breeze right through."],[27.84,"We may finish."],[29.04,"Let's just do it tonight."],[30.24,"No. Um we'll go ahead and segue uh into doing the uh introduction to the book of Revelation, and that'll be it for tonight."],[42.4,"But that's gonna take a little bit."],[44.8,"So strap it."],[46.48,"But uh so um where to start?"],[52.4,"I'll start with uh uh genre."],[55.52,"Okay, so the book of Revelation or the A thepoc Aalpoypcsealypse, which is the Greek title, of St."],[59.92,"John Um apocalypse literally means to uncover something or to reveal something, hence the translation revelation."],[73.72,"Apocalypse does not mean the end of the world."],[78.84,"So uh I know we're used to like zombie apocalypse or nuclear apocalypse, but that's a different that's a derivative use uh that comes from one interpretation of the book of Revelation that's kind of popular in the United States, uh that the book of Revelation is about the end of the world."],[100.84,"Uh so this is a good place to start."],[103.08,"The book of Revelation is not about the end of the world."],[103.96,"is not about the end of the world. A little bit at the end, but most of it"],[105.6,"At least not most of it."],[111.84,"It is revealing something."],[114.64,"Something was revealed to St."],[116.64,"John. He has written it down and is making it known to us."],[122.72,"But apocalyptic literature, or revelatory literature, if you want to call it that, is a genre of literature."],[130.32,"There's other literature like this."],[131.76,"There's other literature like this in the Bible."],[134.72,"So a big chunk of Daniel is apocalyptic literature."],[141.2,"The book of Zephaniah is apocalyptic literature."],[144.0,"I know Zephaniah is everyone's favorite book of the Bible."],[147.36,"But not only is it apocalyptic literature, but it has four chariots pulled by different colored horses."],[157.96,"But anyway, so there are direct parallels."],[161.48,"It's not just that these are kind of similar in genre, but there are direct parallels between other biblical apocalypses and the apocalypse of St."],[170.04,"John. There are also apocalypses that are not in the Bible, but are sort of Bible adjacent."],[180.16,"So one of those is the book of Enoch or 1 Enoch."],[185.52,"We talked about that a little back when we were in the general epistles, because 1 Peter and 2 Peter and Jude reference it."],[192.4,"Jude straight up quotes it at one point."],[196.88,"It's also referenced a lot in St."],[199.44,"Matthew's Gospel, but that's a ways back."],[202.56,"But it is also going to be referenced directly in the book of Revelation."],[207.12,"Some of the imagery and things are going to be sort of borrowed and changed a little."],[213.04,"So the changes are important, or as important as the borrowing when we get to those things and we talk about them."],[237.92,"Before they all kind of stopped reading it as scripture."],[239.92,"But they still had it around"],[242.28,"There was still copied, it was sort of one of those second rank books."],[248.28,"Um and the Apocalypse of St."],[250.84,"Peter actually had uh a great literary career because it inspired a lot of later literature, like Dante's Inferno."],[259.0,"Um so essentially what makes all of these things a genre, what makes all of these books apocalypse, is that there are certain features."],[270.44,"The most major of which is that there is some person who is the receiver of the revelation."],[276.06,"So it's gonna be Saint John here in the Apocalypse of St."],[279.42,"John, but it's generally whoever the book is named after."],[282.94,"So Daniel or or Zephaniah or Enoch in the book of Enoch St."],[288.82,"Peter in the Apocalypse of St."],[290.42,"Peter. That person encounters some kind of spiritual beings."],[299.67,"Usually angelic beings."],[302.55,"Those angelic beings then take that person on a journey through the spiritual world, that includes a journey through the material world because the two kind of overlap."],[325.03,"So the way apocalyptic literature looks at the world is sort of like, and I hope."],[332.19,"I know we've got young people here, so I hope everybody still knows what an overhead projector is."],[337.31,"Because I haven't come up with a better metaphor."],[339.47,"You're looking at me like you don't."],[342.35,"You don't? Okay."],[344.75,"So back in the before time, uh, when we still use telegraphs."],[355.95,"Um before we had the projector that you plug into your laptop to put your PowerPoint presentation on the wall, uh, there were these things called overhead projectors, which was sort of a box, it had a glass surface, a light shined up out of the glass surface."],[374.11,"And then it was okay, now you know what I'm talking about"],[377.59,"And then they had it had like an arm with the overhead part of the overhead projector that had two lenses in it and a mirror."],[387.27,"So the light would shine up through from the bottom, be go through the lenses, go through the mirror, and then get projected on the wall."],[397.75,"And then to project something, you'd had to have to get these clear plastic sheets."],[404.55,"But then those clear plastic sheets were sort of dry erase, like you could write on them, you could draw on them, you could print or type something on them when we had typewriters in the long ago time."],[416.47,"And you would project them that way."],[418.43,"What one of the neat things you could do with them, we thought it was neat at the time at least, was you could get multiple transparencies and sort of layer them."],[429.56,"So you could draw one thing and then sort of overlay another thing."],[435.88,"So now that I've spent all this time explaining what an overhead projector is, probably need to find a better metaphor, because that took longer than just explaining the thing."],[447.08,"But that's kind of how apocalyptic literature views the material and spiritual world."],[453.18,"As sort of there's the material world, and then there's a sort of other transparency overlaid over it."],[459.74,"That's the spiritual world."],[463.27,"And we don't necessarily always, as non saints and non prophets, encounter or perceive the spiritual elements of the world directly."],[477.0,"So what is revealed in apocalyptic literature and revelatory literature is that second transparency is the spiritual world."],[495.32,"Right in the story in Numbers."],[497.88,"Another favorite book of everyone, the book of Numbers."],[502.52,"He's riding his donkey, he's been paid to go curse Israel."],[506.36,"And the donkey keeps turning aside and doesn't want to keep going down the road"],[511.02,"Balaam can't see anything."],[514.26,"And so finally, you know, he's like beating the donkey, trying to get it to keep going, because he thinks the donkey's just being stubborn."],[521.7,"And finally, it's revealed to him the angel of the Lord is standing there getting ready to sort of smite Balaam."],[528.1,"So that's why the donkey had stopped."],[531.08,"And so the idea here is that the donkey is a better prophet than Balaam because the donkey sees what the spiritual reality that Balaam can't see."],[541.5,"But so that's the idea."],[542.78,"So the angel of the Lord is there."],[545.15,"But Balaam can't see him."],[547.79,"Or there's the story of Elisha in uh 2 Kings or Fourth Kingdoms and his servant Gehazi."],[556.49,"Where there's armies coming, they're coming after Elisha."],[560.57,"Gehazi, his servant, is like super scared."],[563.3,"Because there's all these armies coming."],[564.9,"Elisha is not scared."],[566.26,"Gehazi's like, why aren't you scared?"],[569.3,"And so Elisha prays, and Gehazi's eyes are open, and so he sees all the angels."],[574.4,"Like all around on the battlefield."],[578.06,"And so he says, Oh, that's why you're not scared."],[581.2,"Because he can see sort of this other layer."],[583.78,"Elisha is a prophet can see this."],[586.86,"So that's the idea."],[589.26,"The idea is that the spiritual reality is always there."],[590.22,"al It's reveed to someone."],[593.06,"Most people don't see it."],[597.64,"And then that person then is usually commanded to write it down to pass it on to those of us who don't see it normally to give us a perspective."],[608.78,"This perspective of what's going on in the spiritual world that underlies and is interacting with the things that we see physically and materially with our material senses."],[620.46,"So that's the core idea of apocalyptic literature."],[624.46,"That's what is going to happen now with St."],[627.5,"John. And so there are elements of apocalyptic."],[631.36,"This is one of those things, if you need to publish a journal article."],[633.6,"I don't know, the fad may be over now"],[636.28,"In the aughts, back in the aughts, the big fad, if you wanted to publish books and journal articles about the Bible, was to uh write apocalyptic in fill in the blank."],[649.5,"Like you go through St."],[650.38,"Paul's epistles and try to find apocalyptic elements."],[653.1,"You go through some book of the Old Testament, try to find apocalyptic elements."],[657.39,"Um because there are elements of that all through the scriptures, obviously."],[662.77,"People are constantly prophets and other biblical figures, Christ himself."],[666.79,"Are always talking about the spiritual reality that sort of underlies things, that people don't necessarily see."],[674.27,"But apocalyptic literature. is very deliberately that"],[678.87,"It's very deliberately, I'm gonna take you on a tour of the cosmos so that you can see how it all works."],[686.55,"Okay. So, in particular, in the apocalypse of Saint John, I said it's not about the end of the world."],[695.36,"It is this kind of apocalypse, it is this kind of revelatory literature."],[699.65,"So the purpose guiding this is going to be re related to when it's written."],[707.09,"So now I'm gonna go into when it was written and by whom."],[710.93,"So who wrote the book of Revelation and when it was written is actually the most certain of any book in the New Testament."],[719.49,"Because Saint Irenaeus literally told us exactly when"],[726.89,"And Saint Irenaeus of Lyon in the second century was a disciple of St."],[731.85,"Polycarp, who was a disciple of Saint John."],[737.05,"So St. Irenaeus is a spiritual grandson of Saint John the Apostle."],[745.21,"So direct. He would know if anybody does."],[750.3,"And um though he's the only one who gives the exact date, he's joined in saying Saint John wrote it by um by Papius at the end of the first and beginning of the second century, by Saint Justin uh the philosopher, Saint Justin Martyr, um pretty much everyone of that era in the second century, the fathers of that era in the second century, who pretty much all studied in the area around Ephesus."],[781.46,"As we talked about before, in the second century, Ephesus was probably the most major center of the church."],[789.94,"Um and uh so they all studied there."],[795.62,"That's where St."],[796.5,"John was. As we're gonna see, the letters, the sort of cover letters to the book of Revelation, are all written to the Church of Ephesus and other churches around Ephesus."],[807.58,"Other Christian communities and in the towns stuff around Ephesus."],[811.54,"This is where it comes from."],[813.3,"This is where St."],[814.34,"John was. All the fathers in the second century, who are one and two generations removed from St."],[819.62,"John, Papius knew him directly, say he wrote it."],[825.54,"And St. Irenaeus out of them tells us exactly when."],[835.94,"Well, he tells us that St."],[837.38,"John received the vision, I should say, in 95, A.D."],[841.7,"95. Um, so you will usually then, based on that, get it written in 95 or 96."],[853.99,"Because some people will say, well, if he had the vision in 95, he probably didn't have it all written down until 96."],[859.53,"But somewhere somewhere in there."],[862.06,"And this is preserved in the church's iconographic tradition."],[866.62,"All of the depictions of St."],[868.3,"John writing, he's actually dictating to a scribe, and he's an old man."],[872.78,"All the depictions of St."],[874.22,"John going all the way back with Christ."],[877.26,"He's always a very young man."],[880.91,"Um, and we have all the other traditions concerning St."],[884.11,"John. He was the only one of the apostles that wasn't martyred."],[887.55,"Uh he lived to a ripe old age."],[890.29,"And that's that there's an allusion to that tradition, of course, at the end of St."],[894.45,"John's Gospel. Peter, where Christ predicts that St"],[896.73,"The whole thing between him and St."],[900.17,"Peter's going to be martyred by crucifixion."],[904.41,"And then St."],[905.13,"Peter's like, well, what about that guy?"],[906.82,"And Christ says, Well, if it's my will that he lives until I come again, what's that to you?"],[911.94,"And then there's sort of the parenthetical note from St."],[914.9,"John. He says, Because Jesus said that this rumor spread around that that apostle was never going to die, but that's not what he said."],[922.28,"He just said, What's that to you?"],[925.69,"But for that rumor to come around, he must have lived a lot longer than the other ones, or that wouldn't have been a rumor."],[934.73,"So this is this is very clear in the second century that St."],[938.89,"He wrote at that time, that it's the same Saint John who wrote the Gospel of St"],[941.09,"John wrote it."],[946.37,"John. At least the first epistle of St."],[949.33,"John. We won't go into all that again."],[951.23,"This is all the same guy."],[952.75,"Second century, totally clear."],[955.63,"Then everything goes haywire."],[958.72,"Um, because uh who to blame for it?"],[964.48,"Um what happens is that there's a there's a teaching uh that gets started called Killiasm."],[973.6,"Which was the teaching that when Christ returned, he was going to establish a physical kingdom on earth for Christians that would last a thousand years and then the end of the world would come."],[996.92,"Now, but you may be thinking that sounds kind of like premillennialism in evangelical churches."],[1004.76,"It's similar, but it's not the same."],[1007.29,"So for dispensational premillennialists, especially, they believe that the purpose of that thousand year kingdom for Jesus is actually for the Jewish people, for non-Christian Jewish people."],[1022.49,"And this is about fulfilling Old Testament prophecies about ethnic Jews, as if that was a concept of the Old Testament."],[1027.21,"But I show my bias"],[1035.57,"Kileas did not teach that."],[1037.57,"It was Christians."],[1039.17,"Kilias also taught that that thousand years was going to be sort of this wonderful cornucopia of plenty paradise on earth."],[1051.17,"You crush one grape and get a thousand gallons of wine out of it."],[1055.35,"Those kind of analogies are literally used."],[1058.41,"So it's this sort of earthly paradise."],[1062.98,"Uh, it has nothing to do with rebuilding the temple, has nothing to do with all those other things that are associated with sort of Protestant premillennialism."],[1070.54,"They're similar but not the same"],[1070.9,"So these are really two different things."],[1075.3,"Um, so uh, and Killiasm then has its own unique problems."],[1081.16,"Like having this earthly paradise."],[1083.01,"It's about Christians are rewarded, and especially martyrs who were supposed to come alive and enjoy this thousand years on earth where they, you know, eat sumptuously every night and have all these material pleasures to make up for them having died."],[1098.34,"So that ha that that's very problematic from a spiritual perspective."],[1102.28,"Because it's like all that stuff is sort of secondary."],[1107.98,"New heavens, new earth, why."],[1110.27,"Like, um, some of the writings from those traditions end up getting incorporated interestingly into the Quran."],[1119.03,"If you look at the way heaven and paradise are talked about in the Quran, they draw on a lot of that literature, and a lot of the images are very sort of earthly paradise type images, that you find there because they're drawing on some of this literature."],[1132.96,"Okay. So this view was seen as problematic."],[1137.02,"Obviously. They said you're taking these things too literally."],[1140.94,"And uh worse than that, certain heretical groups, so groups that were heretical for other reasons, other than this, were also killeasts."],[1155.79,"Um, that was sort of low on the list of their heretical beliefs, but it was still there."],[1163.08,"In particular, the Montanists, which has nothing to do with the state of Montana, um, the Montanists uh were uh Kiliasm was a huge part of their belief system, and so uh people started calling Montanism because it emerged in Pontus, which happens to be where all these cities are."],[1191.08,"They called Montanism the Pontic Heresy, and they were Kiliasts."],[1196.44,"So what Montanism, the core of Montanism was that there was this fellow Montan whous, it's named after, who traveled around with two female prophetesses, not sus at all, and claimed that he was the promised Pericl."],[1216.72,"That when Christ promised that another comforter would come after he departed, he didn't mean the Holy Spirit at Pentecost."],[1223.2,"He meant Montanus."],[1225.92,"That's who he was talking about."],[1229.44,"And they apparently they did a lot of um trying to figure out how to do this without sounding pejorative again to our Protestant friends, but they did a lot of things related to speaking in tongues and things like that that are associated. now with the charismatic movement"],[1248.52,"Um they obviously the two prophetesses claimed to be legit prophets, and he claimed to be this sort of special being."],[1258.76,"Um they were incredibly morally rigorous, and this is what attracted a lot of people."],[1265.9,"This is something, you know, a lot of times we look at when we talk about the ancient world in this Bible study, we see that very few things have changed."],[1273.42,"This is definitely something that has changed, and I think it's because of Freud."],[1277.82,"But um in the ancient world, especially the Roman world, the stricter and even more brutal your asceticism was in your religion, the more people were. into it"],[1293.03,"People had a super rigorous way of life, they're like, oh, we're in."],[1296.93,"Like St. Augustine even comments on this."],[1298.93,"He's like, man, when it comes to fasting, we got nothing on the maniches."],[1302.05,"Because they would like virtually starve themselves to death."],[1305.33,"They'd be walking around pale and wasted looking."],[1308.15,"Like. And that attracted converts in the Roman world."],[1312.22,"Nowadays, that won't fly, man."],[1314.22,"Nowadays you go and try and evangelize people."],[1317.1,"You know, I don't know how the Mormons get people to give up coffee."],[1319.5,"We were talking about this earlier."],[1320.87,"Like, you know, you try and suggest, hey, you're gonna have to flagellate yourself three times a day, and people are like, yeah, I'm out."],[1327.13,"Like, not so much."],[1329.69,"Um, but that attracted a lot of people. including Tertullian, which is why Tertullian, you may have heard of him as an early Christian writer in the third century"],[1338.61,"He left the church to become a Montanist."],[1343.73,"Because of that."],[1345.73,"It was because he thought the regular Run of the Mill Christians were too wishy-washy."],[1352.75,"They weren't austere and ascetical enough for him."],[1356.53,"Some of them, you know, would go to chariot races and stuff."],[1359.89,"Ugh. So that's um, so he went and became a Montanist."],[1364.66,"That's why he's not St."],[1365.38,"Tertullian. Because he left the church and joined this other group."],[1370.03,"So they, of course, died out."],[1371.63,"But that's who the Montanists were."],[1373.23,"They were Kiliasts."],[1373.87,"And they were heretics associated with this part of the world"],[1382.39,"And when people tried to debate them about Kiliasm, what do you think they pointed to?"],[1391.91,"Revelation of St."],[1393.11,"John chapter 20."],[1396.69,"They said, oh no, see?"],[1399.09,"St. John the Apostle was a Kiliast."],[1401.49,"Look at this."],[1402.53,"Look at this."],[1403.49,"Now, when we get there eventually, it's gonna take us a while."],[1406.45,"But when we get there eventually in St."],[1408.29,"John's Apocalypse, we're gonna see he's not a Killeast."],[1411.57,"That's not what he's talking about."],[1413.21,"Um, but they were pointing at that."],[1415.78,"And add to that that, there were a number of churches had not received the revelation of St."],[1417.46,"John So it was very commonly read in Pontus."],[1428.08,"This area, it's what's now Asia Minor, what's now Western Turkey, basically."],[1433.12,"Um in Alexandria, it was very popular, but that was mostly it."],[1440.64,"So these other churches that hadn't heard of it, the first thing they're hearing about it is it teaches Kiliasm, and those crazy Montanists you like it."],[1453.17,"So they were suspicious of it."],[1456.77,"And there were a lot of people who wanted to reject it because of that."],[1460.77,"Well they had a problem because we have all they had even more than we do."],[1466.54,"All of these second century church fathers."],[1468.7,"So if you're in the third and fourth century, you have all these people closer to the apostles than you, who are all saying, Oh, yeah, St."],[1475.58,"John wrote it, da da da da, and same author and all this."],[1480.27,"So how do you how do you get around that?"],[1483.69,"So, first you just got like, oh no, he didn't write it."],[1487.93,"I mean, you could just deny everything."],[1489.63,"But when you get to someone like Eusebius of Caesarea, um Eusebius of Caesarea was more little who wrote the the history of the church up to the fourth century, wrote the biography of St."],[1501.07,"Constantine. Um he was a little more serious than that."],[1505.45,"He was a scholar of the day in the fourth century."],[1508.66,"So if he's gonna write the history of the Christian church, he can't just hand wave away all of these second century writers who are telling him that St."],[1518.18,"John wrote it."],[1519.54,"So he's he had to try to find a way to separate the book of Revelation from St."],[1528.26,"John's Gospel and the Epistles of St."],[1530.9,"John, which he accepted, because he wants to reject Revelation."],[1534.5,"He's gotta make daylight."],[1536.34,"Okay. And he does this by inventing an imaginary friend."],[1542.1,"And I bring this up because you will still see people citing Eusebius on this this. and arguing for this to day"],[1558.14,"He's motivated here."],[1559.66,"He's not unbiased."],[1562.04,"He's trying to get this daylight there."],[1564.04,"And so he finds this passage in Papias, who, as we said, was writing New St."],[1571.48,"John in his writing at the end of the first or beginning of the second century."],[1576.68,"We've lost the bigger text that this is from."],[1579.56,"But Eusebius quotes it."],[1581.64,"And just working from Eusebius's quote, we can see that this is an imaginary friend."],[1587.72,"But so in one sentence. Papius refers to the apostles he had met"],[1596.88,"He lists them."],[1597.94,"I bet this meant this apostle, but this apostle meant John."],[1602.31,"Then in the next set sentence, he says, and I've passed down all the teachings I received from, and then he lists a bunch of other people."],[1611.77,"But the first person he lists in that other people list is John the Elder."],[1618.17,"John the Presbyter."],[1621.29,"Eusebius jumps on this and says, Aha."],[1625.41,"There are two different people named John."],[1630.86,"So this second guy, this John the Elder, he's the one. who wrote the book of Revelation"],[1638.5,"Now, Papius did not say that this second person wrote the book of Revelation."],[1643.06,"He's not talking about the book of Revelation at all."],[1646.47,"So even if Papius was talking about two different people named John, and it's not like it was a super uncommon name, so there could have been two different people named John."],[1655.65,"That has nothing to do with who wrote Revelation."],[1660.05,"But Eusebius just sort of crafts this John the Elder author of Revelation out of whole cloth to solve his problem."],[1672.85,"No, this was written by this other guy, and we don't know anything about this other guy."],[1676.29,"We don't trust him."],[1677.09,"He's not an apostle, so get rid of that, keep the gospel, keep the epistles."],[1682.59,"I telegraphed this before, but here's what problem with it."],[1686.27,"Remember how 2nd and 3rd John are addressed from the elder, Presbyter."],[1696.27,"So we know this is a title that St."],[1698.35,"John, because Eusebius accepts those two letters as being from St."],[1702.19,"John the Apostle."],[1704.51,"So. So that's a problem."],[1708.0,"There's also the problem that Papius doesn't even say these are two different people."],[1713.52,"He says, Here are the apostles I met."],[1716.96,"End of sentence, period."],[1719.6,"I have passed down everything I was taught by John the Elder, these other people."],[1727.76,"So it's entirely possible, indeed rather likely, I think, based on the testimony of all those other church fathers, that this is the same person."],[1739.28,"That St. John is both one of the apostles that Papius met, but unlike those other apostles he met, Saint John was his teacher."],[1750.72,"Who he spent time with."],[1752.24,"He didn't just meet him."],[1753.86,"In passing. Nevertheless."],[1760.39,"People for the last hundred and fifty years or so, starting mainly with our German friends in the 19th century, uh have seized on this in Eusebius to say try and say, oh, Revelation was written by somebody else, because journal articles have to be published, books have to be written, dissertations."],[1779.92,"Um, but that's all that's all based on nothing."],[1783.74,"So what happens with the book of Revelation then later in church history?"],[1789.9,"Well, uh the book of Revelation ends up becoming canonical based on plagiarism."],[1798.22,"Plagiarism. So quite literally, in both west and east, two acts of plagiarism."],[1805.66,"So uh Saint Jerome was rather. fond of it and translated it into Latin"],[1813.3,"And so he picked up, this is the fifth century, he picked up a commentary of the book of Revelation written by Achilleast."],[1823.14,"Took it, stripped out all the Killeasm, edited out all the Kiliasm, republished it under his own name."],[1832.74,"And that is how the West started to accept the book."],[1837.62,"Western Church began to accept the book of Revelation on Saint Jerome's authority based on his commentary."],[1844.26,"Okay So it happened in the West a little earlier."],[1848.5,"Now we shouldn't think like Saint Jerome published that, and instantly everybody's like, oh good, I'm good with Revelation."],[1853.06,"Put it in the Bible."],[1853.99,"ap Itpened h. over time"],[1855.81,"But that was sort of the turning point."],[1859.46,"Same thing in the East, but even later."],[1862.66,"In the 6th century, St."],[1864.82,"Andrew of Caesarea found this commentary by a Greek fellow named Ecumeneus, who also was Achilleast and wrote a commentary on the book of Revelation."],[1876.26,"And St. Andrew ripped out all the Killasm, republished it started to gain acceptance."],[1890.04,"And so then gradually, over time again, that was the turning point in the 6th century."],[1893.96,"Churches started signing on to the book of Revelation."],[1897.64,"Now, it took a long time in the East"],[1901.65,"Because, for example, in the 9th century, beginning of the 9th century, so that beginning of the 800s AD, St."],[1909.97,"Nikephorus, the Confessor of Constantinople, who was uh suffered under the iconoclast emperors."],[1917.73,"That's why he's the confessor."],[1919.81,"He was the patriarch of Constantinople, and he wrote a list of the books that were canonical, like authoritative, Old and New Testament, and also the books that were sort of Bible adjacent."],[1936.02,"Like some of the books we were talking about earlier."],[1938.56,"He says, These aren't read publicly in the church, but they're read privately."],[1942.76,"Uh and by the way, that's where the word apocrypha comes from."],[1947.64,"The word apocrypha, you've probably heard means hidden or secret."],[1951.16,"That's kind of over translated."],[1953.1,"Because these books are not hidden or secret, like you could go read them."],[1956.92,"It's private as opposed to public."],[1961.64,"You don't read them out in the church, you read them privately, apocryphally."],[1968.68,"So, but he he lists those books."],[1971.32,"And when he lists the Old and New Testament books, he lists here's all the books that all the churches agree about."],[1977.8,"Here's the ones that they disagree about."],[1980.38,"And they're pretty much the ones you do, like with the Old Testament, it's the ones you'd expect."],[1983.98,"Because he's like, okay, some churches aren't using third Maccabees and some aren't using four Ezra"],[1989.23,"So, like those kind of things are in the some people and not others."],[1993.15,"He still includes the Apocalypse of St."],[1995.71,"John in those books accepted by some and not others, in the ninth century, still."],[2001.39,"So it took a long time."],[2003.95,"But this is why you'll hear a lot of folk explanations as to why we don't read the book of Revelation, like in the regular liturgy of the Orthodox Church."],[2012.91,"This is the actual reason."],[2014.91,"It wasn't really accepted until very late."],[2018.11,"And so by the time it was really accepted, our lectionary had kind of taken its form."],[2024.69,"So that's why if you follow the readings, the daily readings for the New Testament in the Orthodox Church, you'll read through the whole New Testament every year, except the Book of Revelation."],[2036.27,"Because it just was accepted by everyone too late."],[2041.15,"That gained sort of universal acceptance."],[2043.23,"It is now universally accepted, in the Orthodox Church."],[2046.13,"That's why it's in the Orthodox Study Bible."],[2048.5,"Is it read in the Roman Church at all in services?"],[2052.18,"I think it is."],[2052.9,"There are some exceptions in the Orthodox Church, too."],[2056.82,"Now we're not in communion with the Copts, but uh the Coptic Church reads the whole thing on uh Posca night."],[2067.3,"All the way through as part of their service."],[2068.82,"Yeah, you think our services are long."],[2071.54,"The Copts just like, I don't know, maybe they save on rent by just living in the church."],[2072.18,"But they have some long services"],[2079.42,"Um but uh and it's read, it's read um on a certain feast day on the Isle of Patmos, which is where St."],[2088.94,"John received the vision."],[2090.62,"Um there are a few places of the Orthodox Church where it's read."],[2094.86,"I think it's in the Western lectionary somewhere, or at least parts of it."],[2099.34,"I don't think all of it is."],[2100.94,"Like, I don't think they have a day where they read about the beast from the earth and the beast from the sea and stuff, but I think like some of the stuff from the letters to the seven churches and some of that is in the um I'm not gonna be able to say sorry, Calvinists as much going through this book because John Calvin. like wrote commentary on the first seven chapters and then threw his hands up in the air"],[2122.98,"It's the only book of the Bible he didn't write a commentary on because he had he literally said after the first seven chapters, like, I don't know what to do with the rest of this."],[2132.42,"I'd be guessing, so I'm not even gonna bother."],[2134.74,"But isn't it your humor literature that on God will wind away the tears from their eyes and there yeah, there are references to it."],[2142.02,"Yeah. Yeah. Um but we never have, you know, the reading is from the revelation of St."],[2150.02,"John. Like on the in the regular cycle."],[2154.39,"Um so that's sort of it has this long road to becoming canonical, which is kind of peculiar."],[2165.29,"So, and if you're really if you really want to nerd out on this, like the magisterial source on this is uh Dr."],[2173.85,"Jeannie Constantinu, who has the show for Axia Faith Radio, her doctoral dissertation was on this."],[2180.25,"The reception history of the Book of Revelation in the East."],[2184.49,"And it's been published it's published in paperback as Guiding to a Blessed End is the title."],[2191.29,"But I understand it's one of those academic books that's very expensive in paperback."],[2200.09,"Kind of prohibitively so."],[2201.45,"So maybe interlibrary loan or something, uh if you're super want to nerd out about it."],[2206.09,"But that she has I mean it's a doctoral dissertation, so it's all there."],[2206.25,"that And as part of, she did a new translation of uh St."],[2215.41,"Andrew of Caesarea's commentary that was published in the Fathers of the Church series that Catholic University Press does."],[2222.61,"Um so yeah, if you want more on that bit."],[2227.14,"Um, but so despite all that, that's all sort of historical circumstance."],[2231.88,"The case, if we just go back to the second century and the people who knew, the people directly connected to Saint John, it's very clear when, and and where by whom it was written."],[2244.39,"When we go back that far."],[2246.39,"So, with that being the genre of literature, that being the time when it's written."],[2253.2,"The place, as we just mentioned, was the Isle of Patmos, which is a place to which St."],[2259.28,"John was exiled."],[2260.64,"So he's not in Ephesus."],[2262.58,"He was exiled there by the Romans."],[2264.1,"The Romans had a salt mine there."],[2267.94,"So it was sort of a prison work colony."],[2271.43,"And uh St."],[2272.79,"John was sent into exile there for a period of years during one of the periodic persecutions of Christians, and that's where he receives this vision."],[2283.11,"Um, and you can go there, there is a monastery there."],[2287.59,"You can go to the cave where Saint John had his vision. uh on Patmos to this day"],[2296.83,"Um so when you take all that together, where is this going?"],[2301.15,"It's if it's not talking about the end of the world."],[2303.77,"And if you think about it, that doesn't make sense anyway."],[2309.09,"Why would Saint John have a vision about something that was gonna happen minimally 2,000 years in the future, as we now know, uh, and send it with cover letters to a bunch of churches in Asia Minor that weren't gonna live anywhere near it."],[2327.99,"Hey guys, I just want to send you a bunch of riddles that you won't be able to understand until Jack Van Impe is born and can explain them to you."],[2334.85,"Like, or fill in the blank, Hal Lindsay, you know, whoever you'd like to use."],[2339.63,"That doesn't make a lot of sense."],[2341.71,"I don't think it makes a lick of sense, but um so this is something he's writing to them."],[2349.55,"We actually have to say."],[2351.09,"Two people alive at the time."],[2353.05,"And so what's being revealed is the spiritual reality behind what is happening at the time, but not only at the time."],[2366.73,"Because in the same way that I said someone like St."],[2370.57,"John in one of these visions would be taken on this sort of tour of the cosmos, that's also not limited to like a snapshot in. terms of time"],[2383.17,"So, like if you read the book of Enoch, Enoch doesn't just get, hey, here's what things are like three generations before the flood or whatever."],[2392.07,"Like Enoch gets taken on this tour."],[2394.07,"He also sees history."],[2395.83,"He sees the history going back to Adam and Eve."],[2398.63,"He sees the history of Israel going into the future in what's called the animal apocalypse."],[2404.15,"Uh, which is not like a meat factory or something."],[2407.37,"It's not me at a barbecue buffet."],[2409.53,"Like the animal apocalypse is there are animals that represent different historical figures."],[2414.67,"Like so, but in there it's the whole history of Israel, up to the birth of the Messiah."],[2419.33,"So there's this is cross-temporal, too."],[2422.61,"St So we have people like St."],[2425.3,"John. John is a leader in these communities."],[2428.52,"Centered around Ephesus."],[2430.84,"They're facing persecution."],[2432.36,"Their leader, St."],[2433.24,"John, has been sent into exile by the Romans."],[2435.64,"Things look bad."],[2439.37,"The Romans are oppressing them, they're martyring people."],[2442.75,"They're trying to live this Christian life."],[2444.91,"They've been told Christ is going to return and establish justice in the world."],[2448.83,"They're not seeing a lot of justice in the world."],[2451.17,"This hasn't happened yet."],[2455.73,"Um, there's debate."],[2457.57,"Probably Domitian. Um so they're they're they're they're facing all this. and St"],[2465.69,"John receives this vision, and this vision shows him how the spiritual element of how the world functions."],[2475.69,"That includes some of the past, regarding Christ."],[2479.54,"That includes some of the future."],[2483.0,"But it's centered on what's going on right then."],[2488.01,"But we're also gonna see Saint John writes this book the same way he wrote his other books."],[2494.33,"Meaning he loops and he cycles."],[2497.98,"So we're gonna see these cycles like of sevens."],[2501.12,"There's seven trumpets, and then when the seventh trumpet blows, they wheel out seven bowls, and then they pour out the seven bowlss, and then there' seven seals."],[2507.52,"These things just keep looping, looping and looping and looping around"],[2514.26,"Because the idea is just like St."],[2517.62,"John said in 1 John, you've heard an Antichrist is coming, and already Antichrist's plural have come."],[2525.64,"Sort of come in that spirit."],[2528.5,"So St. John is going to talk about yes, there are these things at the end of the world."],[2533.46,"There is an end of the world, there's an ultimate end of the world."],[2536.58,"But these things are already playing out now."],[2539.94,"They have been, and they will be until that time comes."],[2546.66,"So it's this is how the world works in this age."],[2551.42,"We've talked before about how, in sort of this New Testament theology, the coming of Christ, his death, his resurrection, his ascension begins what they called beforehand the Messianic Age."],[2562.94,"They were waiting for a Messiah to come, the Messiah was going to inaugurate a messianic age."],[2568.78,"That's sort of the last age before the end."],[2571.6,"So we talked about that's why they refer to it as the last days."],[2574.57,"That these this is the last age before the end."],[2578.57,"And so St."],[2579.61,"John has revealed to him, here's how this age works."],[2584.33,"Here's what's going on backstage."],[2587.18,"Behind what you're seeing play out."],[2591.1,"So that when the people who are facing persecution and to trials and struggles see what's going on behind the scenes and where it's going end up."],[2601.67,"They can take heart, be comforted, remain steadfast in their faithfulness, at the time."],[2610.53,"So if that's the original function, if this vision is given to St."],[2616.69,"John so that he can write it down so that these people can receive it in that way, then that has to guide how we interpret it."],[2626.95,"That it's given to us for a similar reason."],[2630.55,"It's not given to us to predict the future."],[2633.51,"It's not given to us so we can figure out when Jesus is coming back."],[2637.03,"Oops, he died"],[2638.04,"Play pin the tail on the Antichrist."],[2640.84,"Oh, it's Juan Carlos King of Spain."],[2643.8,"Uh someone else in the EU."],[2648.34,"That's not why."],[2649.6,"It's for that same purpose."],[2651.52,"So that we, when we're facing struggles, when we're facing oppression, when we see sort of horror unfolding in the world, economic distress unfolding in the world, when we see these things unfolding in the world and afflicting us, we can know what's going on behind the scenes and where this all ends up."],[2672.74,"So that we can take heart."],[2675.2,"And we can become more steadfast in our faithfulness."],[2679.2,"That's what this. is aimed at"],[2682.6,"And that's sort of the interpretive key for what I'm going to be doing, sort of all the way through, in terms of how we uh interpret and apply this."],[2695.0,"And we're going to begin when we start next time."],[2698.2,"We're going to see the beginning of the vision."],[2700.44,"I don't know how long this is going to take."],[2702.04,"I'm a feeling we're going to move pretty slow to explain things."],[2707.0,"But after that, um, after the sort of the initiation of the vision, we'll get into the letters to the seven churches."],[2714.68,"Um those probably were not all originally attached together."],[2719.88,"Those probably, we'll talk more about this, but those probably were each attached as a cover letter. when a copy of this was sent to each church"],[2730.72,"And so when it was later sort of gathered together to all circulate as a whole, all seven letters were included."],[2737.69,"Sort of in the whole."],[2739.37,"Um, but we'll get more into all those things as we go."],[2745.29,"I won't do those now."],[2746.81,"Um so I think that's it for our introduction."],[2751.53,"Well, there'll be plenty more stuff once we dive in next time."],[2756.01,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2763.37,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."],[2768.57,"That's whole.counsel at ancientfaith.com"]],"o":[{"t":20.64,"lv":2,"h":"Genre: apocalypse = uncovering","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Book of Revelation / <b><i>Apocalypse</i></b> of St. John - \"completely non-controversial book\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Apokalypsis</i> = <b>to uncover, to reveal</b> → hence \"revelation\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not</b> \"the end of the world\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"zombie apocalypse,\" \"nuclear apocalypse\" = <b>derivative use</b></li><li class=\"d1\">comes from one interpretation, popular in the <b>US</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation is not about the end of the world</b> - \"a little bit at the end, but most of it\" no</li><li class=\"d0\">Something was <b>revealed to St. John</b>; he wrote it down to make it known to us</li><li class=\"d0\">Apocalyptic / revelatory literature = a <b>genre</b></li></ul>"},{"t":122.72,"lv":3,"h":"Other apocalypses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In the Bible: big chunk of <b>Daniel</b>; Zechariah (he says Zephaniah) - <b>four chariots</b>, different colored horses</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Direct parallels</b> with Revelation, not just similar genre</li><li class=\"d0\">Bible-adjacent: <b>1 Enoch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">referenced by <b>1 Peter, 2 Peter, Jude</b>; Jude \"straight up quotes it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">also in Matthew; will be referenced directly in Revelation</li><li class=\"d1\">imagery borrowed and <b>changed</b> → \"the changes are as important as the borrowing\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Apocalypse of St. Peter</b> - once copied as \"second rank\" book; stopped being read as scripture</li><li class=\"d1\">inspired later literature, e.g. <b>Dante's <i>Inferno</i></b></li></ul>"},{"t":259.0,"lv":2,"h":"Features of the genre","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Receiver</b> of the revelation - usually whoever book is named for: Daniel, Zephaniah, Enoch, Peter, John</li><li class=\"d0\">Receiver meets <b>spiritual beings</b>, usually <b>angelic</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Angels take him on a <b>journey through the spiritual world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">includes the <b>material world</b> - \"the two kind of overlap\"</li></ul>"},{"t":325.03,"lv":3,"h":"The overhead projector picture","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Metaphor: <b>overhead projector</b> - \"back in the before time, when we still used telegraphs\"</li><li class=\"d1\">glass surface, light up through lenses and mirror to the wall</li><li class=\"d1\">clear plastic <b>transparencies</b>, drawn or typed on (\"when we had typewriters\")</li><li class=\"d1\">could <b>layer</b> several transparencies</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Now I've spent all this time explaining... need a better metaphor\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Apocalyptic view of world: <b>material world</b> + a second transparency, the <b>spiritual world</b>, overlaid</li><li class=\"d0\">We, as non-saints and non-prophets, don't normally <b>perceive</b> the spiritual layer</li><li class=\"d0\">Apocalyptic literature <b>reveals the second transparency</b></li></ul>"},{"t":495.32,"lv":3,"h":"Balaam and Gehazi","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Numbers</b>: Balaam on donkey, paid to <b>curse Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">donkey keeps turning aside; Balaam beats it - thinks it's stubborn</li><li class=\"d1\">revealed: <b>angel of the Lord</b> standing ready to smite him</li><li class=\"d1\">\"donkey is a better prophet than Balaam\" - sees what he can't</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Kings (4 Kingdoms)</b>: <b>Elisha</b> and servant <b>Gehazi</b></li><li class=\"d1\">armies coming; Gehazi scared, Elisha not</li><li class=\"d1\">Elisha prays → Gehazi's eyes opened, sees <b>angels all around</b> the battlefield</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's why you're not scared\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Spiritual reality <b>always there</b>; revealed to someone; most don't see it</li></ul>"},{"t":597.64,"lv":2,"h":"Told to write it down","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Receiver usually <b>commanded to write it down</b> and pass it on</li><li class=\"d0\">Gives <b>perspective</b> to those who don't see</li><li class=\"d1\">what's going on in spiritual world <b>underlies and interacts</b> with what we see materially</li><li class=\"d0\">Core idea of apocalyptic literature - what happens now with St. John</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Apocalyptic in fill-in-the-blank\" - <b>fad of the aughts</b> for journal articles</li><li class=\"d1\">hunt for apocalyptic elements in Paul's epistles, OT books</li><li class=\"d0\">Elements all through scripture: prophets, other biblical figures, <b>Christ himself</b> speak of the underlying spiritual reality</li><li class=\"d0\">But apocalyptic literature does it <b>deliberately</b>: \"I'm gonna take you on a <b>tour of the cosmos</b> so you can see how it all works\"</li></ul>"},{"t":699.65,"lv":2,"h":"Who wrote it and when","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Purpose tied to <b>when it's written</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Author and date = <b>most certain of any NT book</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Irenaeus</b> of Lyon, 2nd c. - disciple of <b>St. Polycarp</b>, disciple of <b>St. John</b> → \"spiritual grandson\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"He would know if anybody does\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>Papias</b> (end 1st / start 2nd c.), <b>St. Justin Martyr</b> - all say John wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">fathers of that era mostly studied around <b>Ephesus</b> - \"most major center of the church\" in 2nd c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Letters/cover letters addressed to <b>Ephesus and churches around it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Irenaeus alone gives <b>exact date</b>: vision received <b>95 AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\">written down 95 or <b>96</b></li></ul>"},{"t":862.06,"lv":3,"h":"Iconography and the old apostle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Iconography preserves it: John writing = <b>old man dictating to a scribe</b></li><li class=\"d1\">depictions with Christ = always a <b>young man</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Only apostle <b>not martyred</b>; lived to ripe old age</li><li class=\"d0\">Allusion at end of <b>John's Gospel</b>: Peter to be martyred by crucifixion - \"what about that guy?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: \"if it's my will that he lives until I come again, what's that to you?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">John's note: <b>rumor</b> he'd never die - \"not what he said\"</li><li class=\"d1\">rumor only works if he <b>outlived the others</b></li><li class=\"d0\">2nd c. totally clear: <b>same John</b> who wrote the Gospel and at least <b>1 John</b> - \"same guy\"</li></ul>"},{"t":955.63,"lv":2,"h":"Chiliasm","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Then everything goes haywire\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chiliasm</b> (<i>Killiasm</i>) - when Christ returns, <b>physical kingdom on earth for Christians, 1000 years</b>, then end of world</li><li class=\"d0\">Sounds like evangelical <b>premillennialism</b> - \"similar, but not the same\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dispensational premillennialists</b>: 1000 years is for <b>non-Christian Jews</b>, fulfilling OT prophecy about \"ethnic Jews, as if that was a concept of the OT\" - \"I show my bias\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Chiliasts: it's <b>Christians</b>; nothing to do with <b>rebuilding the temple</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Chiliast kingdom = <b>earthly paradise</b>, cornucopia</li><li class=\"d1\">\"crush one grape, get a thousand gallons of wine\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems: <b>martyrs</b> come alive, eat sumptuously, \"material pleasures to make up for dying\"</li><li class=\"d1\">spiritually problematic - \"new heavens, new earth, why?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Writings of this tradition later fed into the <b>Quran's</b> paradise imagery</li><li class=\"d0\">Seen as <b>too literal</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1140.94,"lv":2,"h":"Montanists","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Worse: certain <b>heretical groups</b> were chiliasts too - low on their list of heresies</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Montanists</b> - \"nothing to do with the state of Montana\"</li><li class=\"d1\">chiliasm a huge part; called the <b>Pontic heresy</b> - emerged in <b>Pontus</b>, where these cities are</li><li class=\"d0\">Core: <b>Montanus</b> traveled with <b>two female prophetesses</b> - \"not sus at all\"</li><li class=\"d1\">claimed to be the promised <b>Paraclete</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's promise of \"another comforter\" = not Holy Spirit at <b>Pentecost</b>, but Montanus</li><li class=\"d0\">Practices like <b>speaking in tongues</b> - associated now with charismatic movement</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophetesses claimed to be legit; he a \"special being\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1258.76,"lv":3,"h":"Asceticism and Tertullian","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Attracted people: <b>incredibly morally rigorous</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient/Roman world: <b>harsher the asceticism, the more people were in</b></li><li class=\"d1\">changed since then - \"I think it's because of <b>Freud</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Augustine</b>: on fasting \"we got nothing on the <b>Manichees</b>\" - starved, pale and wasted → converts</li><li class=\"d1\">today: \"I don't know how the Mormons get people to give up coffee\" - tell people to flagellate three times a day → \"I'm out\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tertullian</b> left the church to become a Montanist</li><li class=\"d1\">regular Christians <b>too wishy-washy</b> - some went to chariot races</li><li class=\"d1\">why he's <b>not St. Tertullian</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Montanists died out</li></ul>"},{"t":1382.39,"lv":2,"h":"Why Revelation was suspect","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Debating chiliasts: they pointed to <b>Revelation 20</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"John the Apostle was a chiliast!\"</li><li class=\"d1\">when we get there: <b>he's not a chiliast</b> - will take a while</li><li class=\"d0\">Many churches <b>hadn't received</b> Revelation</li><li class=\"d1\">read widely in <b>Pontus / Asia Minor</b> (W. Turkey) and in <b>Alexandria</b> - mostly that</li><li class=\"d0\">Others' first word on it: <b>teaches chiliasm</b> + \"crazy Montanists like it\" → suspicious, wanted to reject it</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: <b>2nd c. fathers</b> closer to apostles all say John wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">3rd/4th c. - \"how do you get around that?\" → deny everything, or...</li></ul>"},{"t":1483.69,"lv":3,"h":"Eusebius and the imaginary friend","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eusebius of Caesarea</b> - 4th c., history of the church, biography of <b>Constantine</b></li><li class=\"d1\">serious scholar - can't just hand-wave away 2nd c. writers</li><li class=\"d1\">accepts <b>John's Gospel and Epistles</b>; wants to reject Revelation → must \"make daylight\"</li><li class=\"d1\">does it by \"<b>inventing an imaginary friend</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">People still cite Eusebius today - \"He's motivated here. Not unbiased\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Papias</b> passage (lost; Eusebius quotes it)</li><li class=\"d1\">one sentence: apostles he'd met - this one, this one, <b>John</b></li><li class=\"d1\">next: passed down teachings from others, first named <b>John the Elder / Presbyter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Eusebius: \"Aha! <b>Two Johns</b>\" → Elder wrote Revelation</li><li class=\"d0\">Papias <b>never says</b> Elder wrote Revelation - not talking about it at all</li><li class=\"d1\">John not an uncommon name - but irrelevant to authorship</li><li class=\"d1\">Eusebius crafts him \"out of whole cloth\": unknown, not an apostle, \"don't trust him\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1682.59,"lv":3,"h":"Problems with the two-Johns theory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 and 3 John</b> addressed from \"<b>the Elder / Presbyter</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Eusebius accepts them as from <b>John the Apostle</b> → title was John's own</li><li class=\"d0\">Papias <b>doesn't say</b> two different people</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Here are the apostles I met. End of sentence.\" Then passed down what he was taught by John the Elder...</li><li class=\"d1\">likely <b>same person</b>, given all the other fathers</li><li class=\"d1\">John both an apostle Papias met and <b>his teacher</b> - \"didn't just meet him in passing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Last 150 years: <b>German</b> scholars (19th c.) seized on Eusebius - \"journal articles have to be published, dissertations\" - \"all based on nothing\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1783.74,"lv":2,"h":"Canonical by plagiarism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Revelation became canonical \"based on <b>plagiarism</b>\" - two acts, <b>West and East</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>West</b>: <b>St. Jerome</b> (5th c.) fond of it, translated into Latin</li><li class=\"d1\">picked up commentary by a <b>chiliast</b>; stripped out the chiliasm; <b>republished under his own name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">West accepted it on <b>Jerome's authority</b> - not instant, \"happened over time\" - turning point</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>East</b>: <b>St. Andrew of Caesarea</b>, 6th c.</li><li class=\"d1\">found commentary by Greek <b>Oecumenius</b>, also chiliast</li><li class=\"d1\">ripped out chiliasm, republished → churches began signing on</li></ul>"},{"t":1897.64,"lv":3,"h":"Slow acceptance in the East","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Long time in East: early <b>800s</b>, <b>St. Nikephorus the Confessor</b> of Constantinople</li><li class=\"d1\">suffered under <b>iconoclast</b> emperors → \"confessor\"; patriarch</li><li class=\"d1\">list of canonical books, + Bible-adjacent books</li><li class=\"d1\">agreed vs disputed books; e.g. some churches not using <b>3 Maccabees</b>, <b>4 Ezra</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still lists <b>Apocalypse of John</b> among books accepted by some, not others</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: <b>apocrypha</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"hidden/secret\" is <b>over-translated</b>; books not hidden</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>private vs public</b>: not read in church, read privately</li></ul>"},{"t":2003.95,"lv":2,"h":"Why Revelation isn't in the lectionary","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Folk explanations why not read in liturgy → <b>actual reason</b>: accepted too <b>late</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lectionary had already taken form</li><li class=\"d1\">read <b>whole NT every year except Revelation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>universally accepted</b> in Orthodoxy; in <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Exceptions</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Copts</b> (not in communion) read whole thing on <b>Pascha night</b> - \"maybe they save on rent by living in church\"</li><li class=\"d1\">read on a feast day on <b>Patmos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Roman Church: perhaps parts, e.g. letters to the seven churches; not the beasts</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calvin</b> commented on first 7 chapters, then threw up his hands - only book without commentary - \"I'd be guessing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Audience: Rev 21 \"God will wipe away every tear\" quoted - references yes, but readings are not \"from the Revelation of St. John\" in regular cycle</li><li class=\"d0\">Long, peculiar road to canon</li></ul>"},{"t":2165.29,"lv":3,"h":"Further reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">For nerding out: <b>Dr. Jeannie Constantinou</b> (Ancient Faith Radio host)</li><li class=\"d1\">doctoral dissertation: <b>reception history of Revelation in the East</b></li><li class=\"d1\">paperback <i>Guiding to a Blessed End</i>; expensive - try <b>interlibrary loan</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also new translation of <b>St. Andrew of Caesarea's</b> commentary - Fathers of the Church series, Catholic University Press</li><li class=\"d0\">All historical circumstance; going back to 2nd c. - clear <b>when, where, by whom</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2246.39,"lv":2,"h":"Place: Patmos","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isle of Patmos</b> - John <b>exiled</b> there by the Romans, not in Ephesus</li><li class=\"d1\">Roman <b>salt mine</b> → prison work colony</li><li class=\"d1\">exile for a period of years in a periodic <b>persecution</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Receives the vision there</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Monastery</b> there; can visit the <b>cave</b> of the vision</li></ul>"},{"t":2296.83,"lv":2,"h":"Written to its own time","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not end of the world - \"doesn't make sense anyway\"</li><li class=\"d1\">vision of something <b>2000+ years off</b>, sent as cover letters to churches in Asia Minor that wouldn't live near it?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Hey guys, riddles you won't understand until <b>Jack Van Impe</b> is born\" / <b>Hal Lindsey</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't think it makes a lick of sense\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Written to <b>people alive at the time</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2353.05,"lv":2,"h":"Not a snapshot in time","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Revealed: <b>spiritual reality</b> behind what's happening then - \"but not only at the time\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tour of the cosmos not limited to a <b>snapshot</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Enoch</b>: not just 3 generations before the flood; sees history back to <b>Adam and Eve</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sees Israel's history into the future: the <b>animal apocalypse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not a meat factory... not me at a barbecue buffet\"</li><li class=\"d1\">animals = historical figures; Israel up to birth of the <b>Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cross-temporal</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2422.61,"lv":3,"h":"The churches' situation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>John</b> leader around <b>Ephesus</b>; exiled by Romans</li><li class=\"d0\">Facing <b>persecution</b>, martyrdom; \"things look bad\"</li><li class=\"d1\">probably <b>Domitian</b> (debate)</li><li class=\"d0\">Told Christ will return and establish <b>justice</b> - not seeing it</li><li class=\"d0\">Vision shows how the <b>spiritual element</b> of the world works</li><li class=\"d1\">some of the <b>past</b>, regarding Christ; some <b>future</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"but it's centered on what's going on <b>right then</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2488.01,"lv":2,"h":"Loops and cycles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">John writes this as he wrote his other books - <b>loops and cycles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cycles of <b>sevens</b>: seven trumpets → seventh blows → <b>seven bowls</b> poured; <b>seven seals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"looping, looping, looping around\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>1 John</b>: \"you've heard Antichrist is coming, and already <b>antichrists</b> (plural) have come\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Yes, things at the end; ultimate <b>end of the world</b> exists</li><li class=\"d1\">but these things <b>already playing out now</b>, have been, will be until then</li></ul>"},{"t":2546.66,"lv":2,"h":"How this age works","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">NT theology: Christ's coming, death, resurrection, ascension begin the <b>Messianic Age</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Messiah to <b>inaugurate</b> a messianic age = last age before the end → \"<b>last days</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">John shown <b>how this age works</b> - \"what's going on <b>backstage</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So persecuted people see behind the scenes and <b>where it's going to end up</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>take heart</b>, be comforted, remain <b>steadfast</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2610.53,"lv":3,"h":"Interpretive key","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Original function guides interpretation → given to us for a <b>similar reason</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not</b> to predict the future; <b>not</b> to figure out when Jesus is coming back</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Play <b>pin the tail on the Antichrist</b>\" - \"Juan Carlos, King of Spain... someone in the EU\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Given so that in struggle, <b>oppression</b>, horror, <b>economic distress</b> we know what's going on behind the scenes and <b>where this ends up</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>take heart</b>, more <b>steadfast in faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Interpretive key\" for all the way through</li></ul>"},{"t":2695.0,"lv":3,"h":"Looking ahead","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Next time: <b>beginning of the vision</b>; going slow to explain</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>letters to the seven churches</b></li><li class=\"d1\">probably <b>not originally attached together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">each likely a <b>cover letter</b> when a copy was sent to each church</li><li class=\"d1\">later gathered together; all seven included in the whole</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's it for our introduction\"</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next: <b>start of the vision</b> (slow going), then the <b>letters to the seven churches</b>"});
